#. 5.4->settings-schema.json->tiling-mode->tooltip
msgid ""
"Off: nothing is tiled automatically. Windows are tiled only when you use the gTile popup, its hotkeys, or the org.gTile.Control D-Bus interface (which is how OhMyDebn's \"Tile with gaps\" keybindings work).\n"
"\n"
"Rule-based tiling: OhMyDebn's own tile-rules.json matches specific apps to specific spots.\n"
"\n"
"Traditional tiling (3 windows): every new window splits into a binary tree (full screen, then left/right, then upper-right/lower-right, alternating vertical/horizontal), capped at 3 windows - a 4th window discards the tree and starts over at full screen; independent per monitor and workspace; ignores tile-rules.json entirely.\n"
"\n"
"Traditional tiling (unlimited): the same binary-tree splitting but keeps going past 3 windows instead of restarting. Both traditional modes automatically rebalance when a window closes, so the remaining windows reclaim the freed space.\n"
"\n"
"Scrollable tiling (like Niri): every new window opens as its own full-height column to the right of the focused one, at the configured column width, and the screen is a viewport scrolling over the strip of columns - focusing a window scrolls its column into view, and columns scrolled out of view are minimized until focused again (window list, Alt-Tab, the column hotkeys on the Hotkeys tab - Super+comma/period by default - or the org.gTile.Control D-Bus methods FocusColumnLeft/FocusColumnRight and MoveColumnLeft/MoveColumnRight). A lone window fills the whole screen and collapses to a column when a second one opens - and expands again when it's alone. Ignores tile-rules.json entirely.\n"
"\n"
"All automatic modes are independent per monitor and workspace, and a window stays in the layout of the monitor and workspace it opened on - moving it elsewhere by hand doesn't re-tile it there. Cycle through the modes with Super+L / Super+Shift+L (see the Hotkeys tab); switching modes only affects windows opened afterwards."
msgstr ""

#. 5.4->settings-schema.json->scrollable-column-width->units
msgid "%"
msgstr ""

#. 5.4->settings-schema.json->scrollable-column-width->description
msgid "Scrollable tiling column width"
msgstr ""

#. 5.4->settings-schema.json->scrollable-column-width->tooltip
msgid "Column width for Scrollable tiling, as a percentage of the usable screen width. At 50% two columns are visible at a time, at 25% four. Widths that don't divide the screen evenly (30%, 40%) leave the remainder empty at the right edge. Only used when Tiling mode is Scrollable tiling. A lone window always fills the whole screen regardless of this setting."
msgstr ""

#. 5.4->settings-schema.json->scroll-animation-duration->units
msgid "ms"
msgstr ""

#. 5.4->settings-schema.json->scroll-animation-duration->description
msgid "Tiling animation duration"
msgstr ""

#. 5.4->settings-schema.json->scroll-animation-duration->tooltip
msgid "How long windows take to glide and resize into place when automatic tiling rearranges them - Scrollable tiling's scrolling as well as the Traditional modes' splits and rebalances - in milliseconds. 0 disables the animation. Automatically disabled while Cinnamon's system-wide desktop effects are turned off."
msgstr ""

#. 5.4->settings-schema.json->maximize-with-gaps->description
msgid "Maximize with gaps"
msgstr ""

#. 5.4->settings-schema.json->maximize-with-gaps->tooltip
msgid ""
"When maximizing a window (titlebar maximize button, Cinnamon's maximize keybinding, or double-clicking the titlebar), leave the existing window-gap margin instead of a true edge-to-edge maximize. Dragging a window to the top edge is not affected - that's a separate top-half tile, not a maximize.\n"
"\n"
"Independent of the Tiling mode setting - applies no matter which mode is selected. Uses the same \"Window gaps in pixels\" size set above.\n"
"\n"
"Note: Cinnamon no longer considers a gap-maximized window \"maximized\" internally, so maximizing it again restores it to its original size and position instead of re-maximizing."
msgstr ""

